\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {XIV}{\ignorespaces High-confidence-spiral robustness rerun. Each row reports the dipole $\sigma $ under \emph  {two} different null hypotheses for the same on-sky data, at NSIDE$\,=\,$64, $N_{\rm  MC}\!=\!1000$, seed$\,=\,$42, full-amplitude convention $p_{\rm  CW}(\hat  n)\!=\!\tfrac  12(1+A\cos \theta )$. The \emph  {monopole-preserving} null draws each subsample's CW counts from $\mathrm  {Binomial}(n_{\rm  pix},p_{\rm  CW}^{\rm  sub})$ \textbf  {using that subsample's own global $p_{\rm  CW}^{\rm  sub}$} (0.49735, 0.49606, 0.49602 for the three rows respectively, not the full-catalog 0.49735 applied uniformly); this isolates the dipole-only signal at fixed monopole. The \emph  {isotropic-$p\!=\!0.5$} null draws from $\mathrm  {Binomial}(n_{\rm  pix},0.5)$; this is the same null construction used elsewhere in the paper but does not preserve the 9.5\,$\sigma $ uniform monopole, so the resulting sigma for any sample with a non-zero monopole is the joint detection of ``monopole $+$ dipole'' rather than just dipole; we report it for like-for-like comparison with the reviewers' request. The HC-cut collapse from $+4.3\sigma $ to $\lesssim \!1\sigma $ is consistent under both nulls. Verification: \artifact  {pipelines/p2\_chirality/outputs/canonical\_provenance/face\_on\_robustness\_results.json} and \artifact  {pipelines/p2\_chirality/outputs/canonical\_provenance/face\_on\_isotropic\_null\_results.json}.}}{37}{table.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:face_on}{{XIV}{37}{High-confidence-spiral robustness rerun. Each row reports the dipole $\sigma $ under \emph {two} different null hypotheses for the same on-sky data, at NSIDE$\,=\,$64, $N_{\rm MC}\!=\!1000$, seed$\,=\,$42, full-amplitude convention $p_{\rm CW}(\hat n)\!=\!\tfrac 12(1+A\cos \theta )$. The \emph {monopole-preserving} null draws each subsample's CW counts from $\mathrm {Binomial}(n_{\rm pix},p_{\rm CW}^{\rm sub})$ \textbf {using that subsample's own global $p_{\rm CW}^{\rm sub}$} (0.49735, 0.49606, 0.49602 for the three rows respectively, not the full-catalog 0.49735 applied uniformly); this isolates the dipole-only signal at fixed monopole. The \emph {isotropic-$p\!=\!0.5$} null draws from $\mathrm {Binomial}(n_{\rm pix},0.5)$; this is the same null construction used elsewhere in the paper but does not preserve the 9.5\,$\sigmaunit $ uniform monopole, so the resulting sigma for any sample with a non-zero monopole is the joint detection of ``monopole $+$ dipole'' rather than just dipole; we report it for like-for-like comparison with the reviewers' request. The HC-cut collapse from $+4.3\sigmaunit $ to $\lesssim \!1\sigmaunit $ is consistent under both nulls. Verification: \artifact {pipelines/p2\_chirality/outputs/canonical\_provenance/face\_on\_robustness\_results.json} and \artifact {pipelines/p2\_chirality/outputs/canonical\_provenance/face\_on\_isotropic\_null\_results.json}}{table.14}{}}
